B.UH=new A.f7("On Uncertainty","Uncertainty is not the absence of knowledge but the presence of multiple possibilities, each weighted by evidence that is itself incomplete. A skilled decision-maker does not wait for certainty before acting; instead, they learn to act reasonably well under ambiguity, adjusting course as new information arrives rather than freezing in place.",B.dW)
B.UB=new A.f7("The Architecture of Habit","Every habit, however trivial it may appear, is the visible result of an invisible structure: a cue that triggers it, a routine that constitutes it, and a reward that reinforces it. Understanding this three-part loop does not automatically make change easy, but it does make the mechanics of change legible, which is often the necessary first step.",B.dW)
B.UG=new A.f7("Technology and Attention","Modern software is frequently engineered, whether explicitly or as an emergent consequence of metrics-driven design, to capture and hold human attention for durations that exceed what the user themselves would consciously choose. Recognizing this asymmetry between design intent and user intent is the first step toward reclaiming deliberate, rather than reactive, use of technology.",B.dW)
